1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:14,035
This is the oracle, your personal research memory. Everybody in the lab has an oracle. A chat window forgets; the oracle keeps what you found, and you can search those memories whenever you like.

2
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:23,398
It stores your key findings, each with the project and experiment it came from, and searches them across all your projects, over the years.

3
00:00:23,398 --> 00:00:39,101
It also searches your lab notebook: your notes for each day, and the prompts you gave Claude Code, which are recorded automatically. Everything lives in your own Obsidian notes, a folder you can open and browse yourself.

4
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:53,278
When you choose, it prepares a finding for the lab oracle, the lab's shared memory, and you publish it. Protected and restricted findings, such as patient data or unpublished results, stay in your own notes.

5
00:00:53,278 --> 00:01:05,452
The bookworm finds papers and the blacksmith runs analyses; the oracle keeps what you found. It remains factual: if it holds nothing on a topic, it says so.

6
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:28,349
A real run. In March, in a project on liver nodules, the small growths in a scarred liver that can turn into cancer, the member asks the oracle to remember that the gene GPC3 marks early hepatocellular carcinoma, the first stage of liver cancer, and tells it apart from a nodule that has not turned.

7
00:01:28,349 --> 00:01:32,879
It files the finding with its project and experiment.

8
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:42,600
Eight months later, in a liver screening project, the member passes six genes and asks what they know about early liver cancer.

9
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:54,300
The oracle recalls GPC3, because early hepatocellular carcinoma is early liver cancer. For the other five, it says plainly that it holds nothing of theirs.

10
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:06,713
To use it, just ask Claude Code: ask the oracle to remember a finding, or what you know about a topic. Give it the finding and where it came from; it asks for anything missing.

11
00:02:06,713 --> 00:02:18,254
The first line of the reply is the verdict: Remembered, Found, Not found or Unsure. You can open every entry in Obsidian.
